president biden was making an urgent  case for voting rights this evening  calling for an exception to senate  filibuster rules as a reminder the  filibuster allows for legislation delays  typically from the minority party in  order to block anything that can't get  up to 60 votes and there was criticism  about his speech today that grew after  several voting rights groups decided to  skip it we're speaking with an  individual from one of those  organizations now latasha brown is the  co-founder of black voters matter fun  and she joins us now latasha thank you  for joining us on quick take i know i  just mentioned you're part of one of the  organizations that chose not to attend  the speech after hearing it do you stand  by that decision  no i think it was the right decision  because i think our point was not to be  let we decided the groups in georgia  that decided not to attend the meeting  we were not we didn't decide because we  wanted to be antagonistic or combative  to the president matter of fact we want  him to actually get this done what we  wanted to do is send a message loud and  clear that this is a serious moment for  us that we feel like a lot of time has  been squandered that we are now at this  moment he himself in his own speech said  that he's been silent long enough that  as a result we feel like we've lost a  lot of momentum and a lot of time and  this is the moment right now that  literally we need more than speeches  that what we need is we need true  commitment and action yeah i i was  actually happy to hear several of the  things that he said in the speech today  but part of the reason why we didn't  attend today is because we said there  were a couple of things one we wanted to  hear  you know it wasn't just a matter of just  hearing what his belief was around  voting rights what we want is we wanted  to know a real clear path forward we  wanted to hear an action plan around how  can we move forward to make sure that  there's a full commitment from the white  house to support voting rights  legislation in this nation what  specifics did you hear in his speech  that made you feel like there is a clear  plan moving forward because he did cite  a lot of the division making it hard to  to get this done  you know one of the things that  activists um and organizers like myself  have been talking about for months now  for months we have we have  met we've gone to the white house we've  expressed this going to the white house  we've met at we went to the senate we've  gone to the house we've met with the  senate leadership the house leadership  we've sent letters to the president  we've we've done everything we've  protested we've been arrested around the  filibuster that for over a year what we  have close to a year we have been saying  that there was no path forward without a  consideration to actually be able to a  man carve out eliminate or whatever you  had to do around the filibuster what was  hopeful that i think was a part of a  plan that i heard from him today was  that he was he has now  publicly expressed which is  distinctively different than what he has  his position has been in recent months  is that he is willing not he is willing  for there to be a carve out or a change  of the rules so that we can move forward  um in spite of the filibuster that he  himself actually called for that and so  i think that was hopeful i think that  that was probably one of the most  significant things that he said in the  speech today sure but he also is dealing  with two democratic senators of course  joe manchin and kirsten cinema who are  not necessarily going to change their  views on the filibuster it's going to be  hard to get them on board  if you were the president what would  your strategy be to get these two  democrats on board with changing the  filibuster so that voting rights can get  pushed through you know i think that  this has to be almost a critical issue i  hope that he continues to weigh in on  this and and literally dig in i hope  that he continues to literally uh call  those senators put continue to put  pressure on them to use what he can use  within the context of his presidential  power i mean some of that he alluded to  today in the speech i'm hoping that he  does that that he actually leverages the  full weight of his office of president  to put pressure on them so that  literally there's a spotlight you know  it's noticeable that he did not call  them out by name and i think that  there's still a space around trying to  negotiate with them you know i have very  very little faith in and particularly  senator madison at this point because i  think he's been extremely disingenuous  and dishonest the entire time which many  of us um feared from the beginning why  why we were calling for the voting  rights um legislation to be a priority  before even build back better or the  infrastructure bill we felt that he was  saying that at the time because he had a  vested interest but not because that he  was actually interested in anyway this  is  center mansion in supporting the voting  rights legislation so i think that  there's a number of things what what i  think what the president reminded us  today that he has a significant history  and um in the senate and that there's an  expectation of someone who who spent  four decades in the senate that i'm  quite sure that there are ways that that  i am hoping that he will use every  pressure point that is available to him  to actually push and to bring those  those senators and hold them accountable  to this their their party to hold them  accountable to the people in this nation  let's talk about the other side here  let's talk about the republicans and  what these restrictions look like  obviously a lot of gerrymandering in  georgia itself if we look in texas  republicans tried to make it easier for  election results to be overturned that  was stopped after democrats had a  walkout i mean there's absentee ballot  limitations tightening of id  requirements the list goes on all of  this revved up after the 2020 election  results why do you think that voting  rights has become such a political  football for republicans  you know i don't think that it's just a  political football i think it's a  strategy i think that they have an  active  con strategy and a concerted effort to  undermine the democratic process that  this is what we're seeing as the best  case scenario we've seen this how  democracies have died all around the  world that this is a best case scenario  for a a minority to actually want to  control the politics of a majority that  the bottom line is they are not they do  not have an agenda that has been  appealing to the majority of americans  of people in this country of different  nationalities different social nomic  backgrounds that literally can actually  for them to hold on to power so what  they've decided instead is that they're  going to cheat that they are willing to  blow up whatever element of democracy in  this country so that a few people could  actually maintain their power and their  positioning i think we have to treat it  as such we can't treat this as this  voting rights issue is just another  policy issue this is another bill that  the republicans and democrats disagree  oh no this is a fundamental issue that  changes the nature and the character of  this democracy in this nation  latasha really appreciate you joining us  and explaining that thank you so much  that was latasha brown co-founder of  black voters matter fund joining us to  talk about president biden's speech on  changing up the filibuster
 

